"""Build source-aligned Version B, exam priorities, and compact rendered-math notes."""
from pathlib import Path
import json, math, re
from scipy import stats
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
DATA=json.loads((ROOT/'data.js').read_text().removeprefix('const DATA=').strip().removesuffix(';'))
B={}
def teach(key,text,legend):B[key]={'paragraphs':text.split('||'),'legend':legend}
teach('start',r'''A population is the whole group you want to describe; a sample is the group actually measured. A population parameter is a fixed, usually unknown feature of that population. A sample statistic changes when you draw another sample.||Read a symbol by its job. \(X\) (random variable) names an uncertain measurement before the outcome is known; \(x\) (a possible value) is one outcome it can take. \(x_i\) (observation number \(i\)) labels a recorded measurement. A bar means average; a square means multiply a quantity by itself.||Start every problem with three lines: “Find,” “Given,” and “Method.” Keep the numbers with those labels as you calculate. A result on the previous line becomes the input to the next line; you do not need to remember it mentally.''',r'\(n\) (sample size); \(N\) (population size); \(\mu\) (population mean); \(\sigma\) (population SD); \(\bar x\) (sample mean); \(s\) (sample SD).')
teach('rbasics',r'''R commands name an operation and its inputs. A vector is a list of measurements; a data frame keeps several variables in labeled columns. Reading the labels tells you whether a number is an outcome, a group code, or a sample size.||An average command gives the center, a variance command gives squared spread, and an SD command restores the original measurement units. The same data can supply all three; the quantities answer different questions.||For exam output, identify what was compared before looking at significance. Find the test name, statistic, degrees of freedom, and probability. Translate the output into an ordinary sentence about the measured groups.''',r'\(\bar x\) (sample average), \(s^2\) (sample variance), \(s\) (sample SD). Actual R commands remain in the separate R reference.')
teach('summary',r'''The mean is the balance point of the observations. To describe spread, measure each observation’s distance from that balance point. Positive and negative distances cancel if you simply add them, so variance uses squared distances instead.||A square changes the units too: squared centimeters are not centimeters. Taking the square root gives standard deviation, a spread in the original units. Sample variance divides by one less than the sample size because the same sample supplied the estimated mean.||SD describes differences between individuals. SEM describes how much the average would change across repeated samples. More observations let positive and negative individual deviations average out more effectively, so SEM shrinks even when the individual SD stays the same.''',r'\(x_i\) (one observation); \(\bar x\) (sample average); \(s^2\) (sample variance, squared units); \(s\) (individual SD); \(\mathrm{SEM}\) (SD of sample means).')
teach('percentile',r'''A percentile is a location in sorted measurements. A high percentile lies near the high end because most observations are below it. The rank tells you where to look; it is not itself a measurement.||Sort first. The course’s rank rule locates the requested position. If it lands between observations, move the corresponding fraction of the gap from the lower observation toward the upper one. A rank ending in three quarters means travel three quarters of that gap.||The median is the middle location. The interquartile range is the distance from the lower quartile to the upper quartile; it describes the middle half without making the extremes determine the spread.''',r'\(p\) (percentile number, e.g. 75); \(R\) (rank); \(I\) (integer rank); \(d\) (fractional rank); \(x_{(I)}\) (sorted observation at rank \(I\)).')
teach('plots',r'''A histogram groups measurements into neighboring ranges, so you can see concentration, spread, and skew. A boxplot marks the median and quartiles, making groups easier to compare without hiding their different centers and spreads.||A Normal Q–Q plot matches sample quantiles to the quantiles expected from a Normal distribution. If the two sets of locations agree after allowing for center and scale, the points follow an approximately straight line. Systematic bending signals a shape mismatch.||Random samples still fluctuate. Read the overall pattern rather than expecting every point to touch the line. A picture describes the evidence and helps assess the distributional setting before you choose a mean-comparison method.''',r'\(Q_1\) (lower quartile), \(Q_2\) (median), \(Q_3\) (upper quartile); IQR (middle-half width).')
teach('classification',r'''Ask what a recorded value means. A measurement such as blood pressure describes an amount. A ZIP code describes membership in a category, even though digits are used to write it. Arithmetic differences between ZIP codes have no measurement meaning.||Interval-continuous values can vary continuously; interval-discrete values count distinct outcomes. The number of tender joints is discrete, whereas measured height is continuous. A measurement rounded to whole years is recorded discretely.||Categorical-ordinal values have an order, such as increasing cancer stage. Categorical-nominal values have labels without a natural order, such as country of birth. This first decision matters because tests for numerical means and tests for categorical counts use different inputs.''',r'A measurement is an amount; a category is membership. Ordered categories do not automatically have equal numerical spacing.')
teach('random',r'''A random variable turns an uncertain outcome into a numerical quantity. If three coins are tossed and you count heads, different sequences can produce the same count. The count is the random variable; the sequence is the underlying outcome.||Probabilities come from equally likely elementary outcomes, not from assuming that every possible count has the same chance. There are more ways to obtain an intermediate head count than to obtain all heads.||List the possible outcomes, group the ones that satisfy the event, and add their probabilities. This makes the probability table concrete before you calculate its mean or variance.''',r'\(X\) (random variable); \(x\) (possible value); \(P\) (probability); \(P(X=x)\) (chance of that value).')
teach('independent',r'''Independence means learning the first outcome does not change the probability of the second. Separate fair die rolls illustrate this: the first roll does not alter the next die.||Drawing cards without replacement is different. Once a king has been removed, both the number of remaining kings and the total deck size change. The second probability must describe that updated deck.||For a sequence, multiply the probability of the first event by the probability of the next event given the first. If the events are independent, the conditional probability equals its ordinary probability. Write what remains before multiplying.''',r'\(P(B\mid A)\) (chance of event \(B\) after event \(A\)); “without replacement” changes the available outcomes.')
teach('pmf',r'''A discrete probability function assigns probability to each possible value. Think of distributing a total amount of one among the allowed outcomes. No outcome can receive a negative amount, and none of the total probability can be missing.||To check a proposed function, inspect signs and then add all listed probabilities. A function that passes one check can fail the other. If an unknown constant scales every probability, requiring the total to be one determines that constant.||The horizontal locations are the possible outcomes; the heights are their individual probabilities. Outside the listed support, the probability function is zero. The values themselves need not be consecutive integers.''',r'\(f(x)\) (probability at value \(x\)); support (allowed values of \(X\)); \(\sum\) (add all listed terms).')
teach('dcdf',r'''A distribution function accumulates all probability at or below a cutoff. Imagine moving from left to right and keeping a running total. Every supported value adds its probability to the total.||A discrete CDF therefore jumps where probability is located and stays flat between those locations. A cutoff between two possible values does not create an extra outcome; it includes only the existing values to its left.||Include probability at the endpoint when the event says “less than or equal.” For a strict “less than” event, exclude any probability mass exactly at the endpoint. This matters for a discrete variable because a single value can carry positive probability.''',r'\(F(x)=P(X\le x)\) (cumulative probability); \(f(x)\) (probability at one value). Uppercase \(F\) accumulates lowercase \(f\).')
teach('expect',r'''Expected value is a probability-weighted balance point. Frequent outcomes pull the balance point more strongly than rare ones. It need not be one of the outcomes you can actually observe.||For a transformed quantity, change each value first, then apply its original probability. Squaring a measurement changes the value being averaged; it does not square the probability.||Keep separate columns for value, probability, value times probability, and squared value times probability. Adding the last two columns gives the mean and the mean square. Those are different because squaring before averaging differs from squaring an average.''',r'\(E[X]\) (mean of values); \(E[X^2]\) (mean of squared values); \((E[X])^2\) (square of the mean).')
teach('variance',r'''Variance measures spread around the expected value. If outcomes cluster near the mean, their squared distances are small. Moving probability farther from the mean makes those distances larger and increases variance.||The direct method weights each squared distance by its probability. The shorter method first averages the squares and then subtracts the square of the average. Both calculate the same spread.||The shortcut is useful because the weighted-mean table already contains most of the work. Keep the mean visible so you subtract its square, rather than accidentally subtracting the mean itself.''',r'\(\mu=E[X]\) (mean); \(E[X^2]\) (average square); \(\operatorname{Var}(X)\) (variance); SD (square root of variance).')
teach('linear',r'''Adding a constant shifts every outcome by the same amount. The center moves, but distances between outcomes do not, so variance stays unchanged. Multiplying by a coefficient stretches distances; squared distances stretch by the coefficient squared.||When independent quantities are combined, each supplies uncertainty. Subtracting an independent measurement does not remove its unpredictability, so its variance contribution still adds. The sign still matters for the mean.||Work the mean and variance on separate lines. Use the original coefficients for means, and squared coefficients for independent variances. Take a square root only if the question asks for SD.''',r'\(a,b,c\) (fixed constants); \(X,Y\) (random variables); independence is needed when adding their variances without a covariance term.')
teach('density',r'''A continuous measurement can fall anywhere within a range. Probability belongs to an interval’s area, rather than to the height at one exact point. A tall narrow region can contain less probability than a shorter wide region.||A valid density never goes below zero and has total area one. Integrating over a requested interval collects exactly the probability in that interval. One exact point has zero width and hence zero probability.||First mark the support, the interval where the density can be nonzero. Limits outside that support contribute no area. Keep density height, cumulative area, and the measurement cutoff labeled separately.''',r'\(f(x)\) (density height); \(\int_a^b f(x)\,dx\) (area/probability between cutoffs \(a,b\)); support (allowed interval).')
teach('uniform',r'''A Uniform density is flat over its support, so equal-width intervals have equal probability. Widening the whole support spreads the same total probability over more space and lowers the density height.||An interval’s probability is its width divided by the support’s width. The mean is the midpoint because equal amounts of density lie on either side. The wider the support, the more spread the outcomes have.||Draw the support before taking a width ratio. Only the portion of the requested interval inside the support contributes probability. A single-point probability is still zero.''',r'\(a\) (lower support endpoint); \(b\) (upper support endpoint); \(b-a\) (full width); \(X\) (Uniform measurement).')
teach('integrate',r'''Integration adds many thin pieces of area. For a density, each piece has height given by the density and a tiny width. An antiderivative is a convenient way to add those pieces over a whole interval.||To determine an unknown density constant or endpoint, require all the supported area to total one. Then solve the resulting equation and check that its answer fits the stated support and nonnegative density.||Write the antiderivative before inserting limits. Evaluate it at the upper endpoint, then subtract its value at the lower endpoint. Keeping those two evaluations separate prevents a lower-limit sign from disappearing.''',r'\(\int_a^b f(x)\,dx\) (accumulated area); \(c\) (unknown constant or endpoint, as defined in the question).')
teach('ccdf',r'''A continuous CDF is an area meter: at a cutoff, it reports how much density has accumulated to the left. It starts at zero before the support and reaches one after all supported values have been included.||Inside the support, integrate from its lower endpoint to the cutoff. Use a different integration symbol for the moving measurement so the cutoff stays a fixed endpoint. The CDF cannot decrease because accumulated probability cannot be taken away.||For an interval, subtract the area accumulated before the lower cutoff from the area accumulated by the upper cutoff. For a median, find the cutoff with half the area to its left. These are two uses of the same CDF.''',r'\(u\) (integration variable); \(x\) (cutoff); \(F(x)\) (area up to cutoff); \(m\) (median cutoff).')
teach('cexpect',r'''A continuous mean is also a balance point. Instead of summing value times probability over isolated outcomes, multiply value by density and integrate over the support. More density in a region gives that region more influence.||For the mean square, square the value before multiplying by density. Subtracting the square of the mean gives variance. This is the same reasoning as the discrete weighted table, with an integral replacing a sum.||For an increasing density, more probability sits toward the higher endpoint, so the mean lies above the midpoint. The median divides area in half; it does not have to equal the mean. Check whether the calculated locations fit that picture.''',r'\(E[X]\) (density-weighted mean); \(E[X^2]\) (density-weighted mean square); \(\mu\) (mean).')
teach('normal',r'''A Normal distribution has a bell shape centered at its mean. Moving the mean shifts the whole curve. Increasing SD spreads the curve out while keeping its total area one, so the peak becomes lower.||Standardizing measures a value’s distance from the mean in SD units. A value below the mean has a negative standardized value; one above has a positive value. Different Normal measurements can then share the same standard Normal table.||The second entry in the course’s Normal notation is variance. The standardization denominator is SD, the square root of that entry. Label both before substituting to avoid mixing squared and original units.''',r'\(\mu\) (Normal mean); \(\sigma\) (SD); \(\sigma^2\) (variance); \(Z\) (standard Normal variable); \(z\) (standardized value).')
teach('normalprob',r'''A Normal probability asks how much area lies in a specified region. Shade that region first: left of a cutoff, right of a cutoff, or between two cutoffs. This determines whether you use the cumulative area directly, subtract it from one, or subtract two cumulative areas.||Convert each measurement cutoff into SD units. A standard Normal cumulative table gives area to the left. Subtract the lower cumulative area from the higher cumulative area for a middle interval.||After calculating, compare with the shaded picture. A small upper tail should not become a probability near one. For a probability about an average, change the spread to the mean’s SE before standardizing.''',r'\(\Phi(z)\) (standard Normal area to the left); \(z\) (distance from the mean in SD units); probability is dimensionless.')
teach('normalquant',r'''A percentile reverses an area question. You know how much probability should lie to the left and want the measurement cutoff that produces that area. A high percentile should land above the mean.||Find the standard Normal location for the requested cumulative area. Multiplying by SD converts the distance into the original units; adding the mean places it at the right location on the measurement scale.||Keep percentile number and probability distinct. A percentile is specified using a number out of one hundred; the inverse distribution uses its probability as a fraction of one. The result is a measurement, not an area.''',r'\(p\) (left-tail probability); \(z_p\) (standard Normal cutoff with that area); \(x_p\) (cutoff in measurement units).')
teach('sampling',r'''A new random sample gives a new mean. The distribution of all those possible means is the sampling distribution. It describes uncertainty in an average, rather than variation among the individual observations inside one sample.||Averaging lets individual high and low deviations partly cancel. Larger independent samples therefore have less variable means, even if individual measurements stay equally scattered. The expected sample mean still sits at the population mean.||If individuals are Normal, means are Normal at any sample size. The central limit theorem gives approximately Normal means for sufficiently large independent samples from other populations; the course uses its stated large-sample guideline. For a question about an average, standardize using the mean’s spread.''',r'\(\bar X\) (random sample mean); \(\sigma\) (individual SD); \(\sigma/\sqrt n\) (SD of sample means); \(n\) (observations per sample).')
teach('estimators',r'''An estimator is a rule for turning sample data into a guess about a population. Unbiased means its repeated-sampling balance point is correct; it does not mean one particular sample lands exactly on the truth.||Precision concerns how tightly repeated estimates cluster. Increasing sample size narrows the distribution of means because more observations average out some individual variation. The decrease follows a square root, so doubling the sample size does not halve SEM.||The course also gives an estimated SE for sample variance in its Normal setting. This describes uncertainty in a variance estimate, a different object from individual SD or SEM. Always name the statistic whose uncertainty is being requested.''',r'\(s^2\) (estimated variance); \(\operatorname{SE}(s^2)\) (uncertainty of that variance estimate); \(n\) (sample size).')
teach('distributions',r'''A test statistic changes across samples even when the null hypothesis holds. Its reference distribution shows which values are ordinary under that null and which would be unusually extreme.||The t distribution is symmetric; using a sample SD introduces extra uncertainty, especially with few degrees of freedom. F and chi-square statistics are nonnegative and look for unusually large ratios or accumulated discrepancies.||Choose the reference distribution and degrees of freedom from the method. For a two-sided t test, both signs can signal a difference, so compare the magnitude with a positive two-tail cutoff. F and chi-square use upper-tail cutoffs.''',r'\(\nu\) (degrees of freedom); \(\alpha\) (significance level); \(|t|\) (t magnitude); df (degrees of freedom).')
teach('choose',r'''Read the outcome before choosing a test. A numerical measurement leads to a question about means. A categorical outcome leads to a question about event proportions or category counts. Group labels themselves are not the numerical outcome.||For a numerical outcome, ask whether one sample is being compared with a fixed reference, two independent groups are compared, or several balanced groups are compared. That separates one-sample t, pooled t, and balanced ANOVA.||For counts, distinguish one observed category list versus target probabilities from a cross-classification of groups and outcomes. Then identify whether a two-by-two continuity correction and a multiple-comparison correction are needed. The named event and the requested comparison determine the final sentence.''',r'Numerical outcomes: means. Categorical outcomes: counts/proportions. A reference mean is one number, not a second observed sample.')
teach('hypothesis',r'''A null hypothesis states the population comparison, such as equal means or equal event proportions. Sample estimates can differ even under that null because a random sample fluctuates. The test asks whether the observed difference is large relative to that expected fluctuation.||The statistic divides a discrepancy by its sampling noise, or accumulates standardized count discrepancies. A larger statistic is stronger evidence only according to the method’s appropriate tail or tails.||State the null, calculate the statistic, identify degrees of freedom, and compare with a critical value or p value. Rejecting supports a difference. Failing to reject means a difference was not established; it is not a demonstration that the population values are identical.''',r'\(H_0\) (null comparison); \(H_1\) (alternative); \(\alpha\) (decision threshold); p value (tail probability under \(H_0\)).')
teach('anova',r'''ANOVA asks whether the group means are farther apart than ordinary individual scatter can explain. Within-group variance describes that individual scatter. If means separate while within-group noise stays fixed, the F ratio rises.||Under equal population means, group means still vary because each comes from a sample. Multiplying the variance of those means by the common group size puts it on the same variance scale as within-group scatter. The ratio then compares two estimates of the same noise under the null.||For balanced groups, average the group variances for within variance, then compute variance across group means and multiply by the common group size for between variance. A large ratio supports an overall mean difference. A separate adjusted comparison identifies which requested pairs differ.''',r'\(m\) (number of groups); \(n\) (subjects per group); \(s_{\mathrm{wit}}^2\) (within-group noise); \(s_{\mathrm{bet}}^2\) (between-group estimate); \(F\) (between/within ratio).')
teach('anovaSS',r'''The sum-of-squares view separates the variation of observations around their own group means from the variation caused by group centers being apart. Dividing these components by their degrees of freedom gives variance estimates.||The comparison has the same intuition as balanced ANOVA: separation among centers is compared with ordinary scatter within groups. An R ANOVA table reports these components in its standard table.||For this midterm’s hand work, use the balanced variance recipe. The review excludes manual sum-of-squares ANOVA. Reading the F statistic, degrees of freedom, and probability in R output remains useful.''',r'\(F\) (between-group estimate divided by within-group estimate). Manual sum-of-squares work is outside this midterm.')
teach('pooledt',r'''Two independent sample means can differ because their population means differ, because sampling fluctuates, or both. The pooled t procedure measures the mean difference in units of its own expected sampling noise.||Assuming equal population variances, combine the two sample variance estimates. The larger sample contributes more degrees of freedom, so it contributes more information to this pooled estimate. The SE then depends on both group sizes.||Subtract the means in a declared order and divide by the SE of that difference. The sign records direction; a two-sided decision uses the magnitude. Keep the pooled variance, SE, statistic, and degrees of freedom on different lines.''',r'\(s_p^2\) (pooled variance); \(\bar x_1,\bar x_2\) (group means); \(n_1,n_2\) (group sizes); \(\operatorname{SE}_{\Delta}\) (SE of their difference).')
teach('onet',r'''A one-sample t test compares an estimated population mean with a fixed reference. The sample mean can miss the reference by ordinary sampling variation, so the raw difference alone is not enough to judge the evidence.||SEM gives the scale of variation expected in sample means. Dividing the reference difference by SEM expresses how many mean-error units separate the observation from the null value. Smaller SEM makes the same raw difference more compelling.||Use the sample SD to build SEM, then compare the signed statistic’s magnitude with a two-sided t cutoff when the question asks “different.” The fixed reference contributes no second sample size or second variance estimate.''',r'\(\mu_0\) (fixed reference mean); \(\bar x\) (observed mean); \(s\) (sample SD); \(s/\sqrt n\) (SEM); \(n-1\) (df).')
teach('posthoc',r'''An overall ANOVA can show that some means differ without naming a pair. A follow-up comparison asks how far apart two selected means are relative to the shared noise already estimated in that ANOVA.||Reuse the within-group variance from all groups. For balanced groups, every pair has the same SE denominator, so differences in the statistics come from differences between means. Keep the ANOVA denominator degrees of freedom too.||Count the comparisons actually requested. All pairs and treatment-versus-control comparisons are different families. The family size controls how strict the adjusted decision must be.''',r'\(i,j\) (the selected groups); \(s_{\mathrm{wit}}^2\) (ANOVA noise); \(k\) (requested comparisons); \(N\) (total subjects).')
teach('bonferroni',r'''Testing more pairs creates more opportunities for a chance finding. Bonferroni keeps the family’s total significance budget fixed by giving each requested comparison a smaller share.||Divide the overall significance level by the number of comparisons. For a two-sided t comparison, divide that individual level between two tails. The resulting cutoff is larger, so only sufficiently strong differences pass.||Every pair in that family uses the same adjusted level. A treatment-versus-control question counts only those control comparisons, even if many additional pairs could have been formed.''',r'\(\alpha_T\) (overall level); \(k\) (requested comparison count); \(\alpha_T/k\) (individual two-tail level).')
teach('holm',r'''Holm spends the same significance budget in an ordered sequence. Start with the strongest evidence, because that comparison must pass the strictest threshold. Keep the pair’s name attached as you reorder.||After a rejection, fewer undecided comparisons remain, so the next threshold can be less strict. At the first failure, stop: the remaining comparisons are not rejected. A later, larger individual threshold cannot restart the sequence.||Sort p values from smallest upward. If all comparisons share the same reference distribution and degrees of freedom, larger statistic magnitudes correspond to smaller p values, so the course can sort those magnitudes instead.''',r'\(k\) (family size); \(j\) (current rank, beginning at 1); \(\alpha_T\) (overall level).')
teach('trials',r'''Random assignment makes treatment groups comparable by preventing a systematic assignment rule from selecting a particular type of patient into one treatment. It concerns who receives what. Masking concerns who knows what was received.||In a parallel trial, different people receive different treatments. In a crossover, a person receives treatments in different periods and serves as their own control. A stable condition and avoidance of carryover matter because an earlier treatment can influence a later measurement.||A protocol defines the question, eligibility, outcome, and procedure before the trial. Recruitment and adherence affect the data actually obtained. Read these design terms as descriptions of what happens to participants, rather than as interchangeable labels.''',r'Randomization (assignment); masking/blinding (knowledge of assignment); crossover (the same person receives treatments across periods).')
teach('binary',r'''Code the chosen event as one and its absence as zero. Adding those observations counts events; dividing by the number of people gives the event fraction. Thus a proportion is also the mean of a binary variable.||Squaring a zero or one leaves it unchanged. This makes the binary mean square equal to the event probability, and the variance becomes probability times its complement. Event coding determines which probability you are describing.||The estimated proportion has less sampling variation in a larger independent sample. Divide the estimated binary variance by sample size, then take the square root for the proportion’s SE. Individual binary spread and uncertainty in its estimated proportion are different quantities.''',r'\(m\) (event count); \(n\) (total people); \(\hat p\) (sample event proportion); \(1-\hat p\) (non-event proportion).')
teach('zprop',r'''A two-proportion test asks whether the populations have the same event probability. Under that null, both groups estimate one common probability, so combine their event counts and total people. Do not give a small group the same weight as a large one by averaging their fractions.||The pooled probability supplies the binary variance under the null. Both independent samples contribute uncertainty to the difference, so their reciprocal-size contributions add inside its SE.||For this course’s two-by-two comparisons, shrink the absolute observed gap by the continuity correction before dividing by that SE. Keep the two proportions, pooled proportion, correction, and SE visibly labeled; then compare the corrected magnitude with the Normal cutoff.''',r'\(m_1,m_2\) (event counts); \(n_1,n_2\) (totals); \(\hat p\) (pooled null proportion); \(\operatorname{SE}_0\) (null SE of the difference).')
teach('yates',r'''Counts change in whole-person steps, while the reference curve is continuous. The course uses a half-count continuity adjustment for two-by-two comparisons to reduce the apparent discrepancy before judging it against that curve.||For a proportion difference, the half-count adjustment becomes half the sum of reciprocal group sizes. For a chi-square cell, subtract half a count from the absolute observed–expected gap, stopping at zero. Then square the adjusted gap.||The corrected z magnitude squared equals the corrected chi-square statistic for the same two-by-two table. That identity is a useful check on hand arithmetic. Larger tables use the ordinary cell gaps in this course.''',r'\(O\) (observed cell count); \(E\) (expected cell count); continuity correction (half-count adjustment); \(\operatorname{SE}_0\) (pooled null SE).')
teach('contingency',r'''An expected count is what a cell would average under the null model, while keeping the observed row and column totals. If groups share the same outcome proportions, every row should distribute its people across outcomes in the overall column proportions.||Multiply a row total by a column’s fraction of all people. This gives the cell’s expected allocation. It can be fractional because it is a model-based average, rather than a newly counted set of people.||Check the margins after building the expected table. Expected counts must add back to each row total, each column total, and the grand total. A mistake here affects every later cell contribution.''',r'\(R_i\) (row total); \(C_j\) (column total); \(N\) (grand total); \(E_{ij}\) (expected count in row \(i\), column \(j\)).')
teach('chi',r'''A chi-square contingency statistic combines discrepancies across the table. Squaring prevents positive and negative cell gaps from cancelling. Dividing by expected count expresses a gap relative to the size of count expected there.||Add all cell contributions. A large total means the outcome pattern differs substantially from the common-proportions pattern. For a two-by-two table, use the course’s corrected gaps; for a larger table, use uncorrected gaps.||The degrees of freedom depend on the independent ways cells can vary after margins are fixed. Compare the statistic with an upper-tail cutoff. For several groups, a significant overall result can be followed by the specifically requested adjusted two-by-two comparisons.''',r'\(O_{ij}\) (observed cell); \(E_{ij}\) (expected cell); \(r,c\) (numbers of rows and columns); \(\chi^2\) (sum of standardized squared gaps).')
teach('gof',r'''Goodness of fit starts with one observed category list and a proposed probability list. There is no second treatment row. The question is whether the observed allocation resembles those specified probabilities.||Multiply each proposed probability by the total number of observations to get its expected count. The same squared-gap-over-expected idea then measures discrepancies in each category and combines them.||If more observations move away from the specified pattern, the statistic grows. Use one fewer degree of freedom than the number of categories when the probabilities are specified, as in the course example. State the conclusion about the overall category distribution.''',r'\(p_i\) (specified category probability); \(N\) (total observations); \(E_i\) (expected category count); \(k\) (number of categories).')
teach('risk',r'''Risk is the event fraction among all people in a group. Relative risk divides one group’s risk by the comparison group’s risk. A value below one means the event is less frequent in the numerator group.||Absolute risk change asks a different question: how many events per person are avoided or added. Subtract the two risks for that change; dividing them describes a relative change. Both begin with the same clearly labeled event fractions.||Prospective groups can provide event risks. A case-control sample selects people by disease status, so its case fraction is set by the sampling design. Use the course’s odds comparison for that design instead of treating that sample fraction as population disease risk.''',r'\(r_T\) (treated/exposed event risk); \(r_C\) (control risk); RR (risk ratio); ARR (control risk minus treated adverse-event risk).')
teach('nnt',r'''An absolute event reduction gives the average benefit per treated person. Its reciprocal asks how many people must be treated for one additional avoided event compared with control. A larger benefit per person therefore gives a smaller NNT.||For harm, use the absolute increase in adverse-event risk and take its reciprocal. Decide whether the event is desirable or adverse before naming a benefit or harm. Keep risks as fractions of one in the calculation.||A fractional ratio describes the average comparison. If asked for a whole-person count, round upward. The interpretation belongs to the study’s outcome and follow-up period, because those define the event risk being compared.''',r'ARR (absolute adverse-event reduction); ARI (absolute adverse-event increase); NNT (number needed to treat); NNH (number needed to harm).')
teach('odds',r'''Odds compare events with non-events, while risk compares events with everyone. Those denominators differ. An odds ratio compares two such event-to-non-event balances, keeping the same outcome and group direction.||Writing the two odds as fractions shows why diagonal products work: dividing the fractions multiplies one numerator by the other denominator. Label the table before using that shortcut. Reversing only one comparison reverses the ratio.||For case-control data, compare exposure odds among cases with exposure odds among controls. This uses quantities the design actually supplies. State the result as an odds comparison and name which group has the higher or lower odds.''',r'\(a,b\) (event/non-event counts in exposed row); \(c,d\) (event/non-event counts in unexposed row); OR (odds ratio).')
teach('rcode',r'''A probability command and an inverse-probability command travel in opposite directions. The first starts with a measurement cutoff and returns area. The second starts with area and returns a measurement cutoff. Both require SD, not variance, as their spread input.||A test output condenses the same method as hand work. Identify the test, statistic, degrees of freedom, and p value before deciding what population comparison it addresses. For the course’s pooled t procedure, check the equal-variance setting.||For count tables, the course’s two-by-two continuity correction must match the procedure used. Translate the final output into the same contextual decision sentence you would write after a hand-calculated statistic.''',r'\(p\) (tail probability in output); df (degrees of freedom); \(F,t,\chi^2\) (test statistics).')
teach('power',r'''A decision can be wrong because samples fluctuate. A Type I error rejects a null that is true. A Type II error misses a specified difference when the null is false. These are statements about the true situation and the resulting decision.||Power is the chance of detecting the specified difference. Larger effects are easier to separate from noise; greater variability makes separation harder. Sample size and the decision cutoff also change the chance of detection.||This collection remains available for later review. Session 15 explicitly excludes Session 14 from this midterm, so the included probability and comparison methods take priority now.''',r'\(\alpha\) (Type I error probability); \(\beta\) (Type II error probability); \(1-\beta\) (power).')
