# All worked-example inputs below are from uploaded lectures/homework, never invented exam claims.
block('language','Read the symbols without holding them in memory',para(r'\(X\) (random variable); \(x_i\) (one observation); \(\bar x\) (sample mean); \(\mu\) (population mean); \(s\) (sample SD); \(\sigma\) (population SD); \(s^2,\sigma^2\) (variances, squared units); \(n\) (sample size); \(m\) (groups, except when explicitly event count); \(\nu\) (df). A bar means average; a hat means estimate; \(\sum\) means add. '+bold('Variance is not SD. Square SD for a variance; square-root variance for SD.')), 'Review pp. 3–4, 27, 31')
block('classify','Choose the outcome scale first',para(bold('Interval-continuous:')+' height, LDL, blood pressure. '+bold('Interval-discrete:')+' tender-joint count; age recorded in whole years. '+bold('Nominal:')+' ZIP code, country of birth (labels; no amount/order). '+bold('Ordinal:')+' cancer stage or pain category (ordered; gaps need not be equal). '+bold('No arithmetic meaning just because digits appear.')), 'Review p. 8; HW1 Q1')
block('describe','Raw-data summary: freshman heights',para(r'Given \(65,69,59,64,62,67,68,62,64,65\) inches; \(n=10\). '+bold('1. Mean:'))+eq(r'\bar x=\frac{645}{10}=64.5')+para(bold('2. Subtract the mean, then square each distance:'))+eq(r'\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x_i&65&69&59&64&62\\x_i-\bar x&.5&4.5&-5.5&-.5&-2.5\\(x_i-\bar x)^2&.25&20.25&30.25&.25&6.25\end{array}')+eq(r'\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x_i&67&68&62&64&65\\x_i-\bar x&2.5&3.5&-2.5&-.5&.5\\(x_i-\bar x)^2&6.25&12.25&6.25&.25&.25\end{array}')+para(bold('3. Add the squared column; 4. divide; 5. square-root:'))+eq(r'\sum(x_i-\bar x)^2=82.5')+eq(r's^2=\frac{82.5}{9}=9.1667,\quad s=\sqrt{9.1667}=3.0277')+eq(r'\mathrm{SEM}=\frac{3.0277}{\sqrt{10}}=0.95743\ \text{in.}')+para(r'SD (individual spread) is \(3.0277\) inches; SEM (uncertainty in the mean) is \(0.95743\) inches. '+bold('Whole population:')+r' use \(N\) and \(\mu\), dividing squared distances by \(N\). '+bold('Quick arithmetic check:'))+eq(r'\sum(x_i-\bar x)^2=\sum x_i^2-\frac{(\sum x_i)^2}{n}')+eq(r'41685-\frac{645^2}{10}=82.5'),'HW1 Q4; review pp. 3–4, 31')
block('percent','Median, quartiles, interpolation',r'''<p>Sort first. The senior sample sorts to \(66,67,69,69,70,70,71,71,72,72\). For the 75th percentile:</p>\[R=1+\frac{75(10-1)}{100}=7.75\]\[x_{.75}=x_{(7)}+.75(x_{(8)}-x_{(7)})\]\[=71+.75(71-71)=71\ \text{in.}\]<p>Rank \(7.75\) is a location; \(71\) inches is the answer. Even sample: average the two middle values, giving median \(70\).</p>\[Q_1=69,\quad \mathrm{IQR}=71-69=2\]<p>Range endpoints are \([66,72]\); numerical width is \(6\). Q–Q: approximately straight means Normal shape is plausible; systematic curvature suggests a mismatch.</p>''','HW1 Q4; review pp. 6–7')
block('discrete','Discrete: validity → CDF → moments',para(bold('Checks:')+r' every probability is nonnegative, and the total is one. HW1’s \(.4,.1,.4\) example totals \(.9\), so it fails. '+bold('Worked probability table (HW1):'))+eq(r'\begin{array}{r|rrrr}x&-1&2&3&7\\f(x)&.55&.15&.10&.20\\xf(x)&-.55&.30&.30&1.40\\x^2f(x)&.55&.60&.90&9.80\end{array}')+eq(r'\sum f(x)=1,\quad E[X]=-.55+.30+.30+1.40=1.45')+eq(r'E[X^2]=.55+.60+.90+9.80=11.85')+eq(r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=11.85-(1.45)^2=9.7475')+para(r'\(E[X^2]\) (average square) differs from \((E[X])^2\) (squared average). Square values, not their probabilities. General transformed mean:')+eq(r'E[h(X)]=\sum_x h(x)f(x)')+para(bold('CDF:')+' add masses through the cutoff; the graph is a staircase:')+eq(r'F(x)=\begin{cases}0&x<-1\\.55&-1\le x<2\\.70&2\le x<3\\.80&3\le x<7\\1&x\ge7\end{cases}')+para(r'For example, \(F(2)=.70\), while \(P(X<2)=.55\). At a jump, equality matters. '+bold('Sketch:')+' isolated probability bars at supported values; CDF flat between jumps, filled at the new height.'),'HW1 Q2; review pp. 9–15')
block('depend','Count outcomes; update dependent probabilities',para(r'Three fair coin tosses have eight equally likely sequences. Exactly two heads occurs as HHT, HTH, THH:')+eq(r'P(X=2)=\frac38')+para(r'Two kings without replacement: after the first king, only three kings remain among 51 cards.')+eq(r'P(\text{two kings})=\frac4{52}\frac3{51}=0.0045249')+para(r'Independence leaves the second probability unchanged. Without replacement, update what remains. General sequence rule:')+eq(r'P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B\mid A)'),'Session 3 pp. 11, 14–16')
block('continuous','Continuous density: area, not height',para(r'\(f(x)\) is density height, not point probability. It is nonnegative and integrates to one. The lecture example is \(f(x)=x/2\) for \(0\le x\le2\). '+bold('1. Check total area:'))+eq(r'\int_0^2\frac{x}{2}\,dx=\left[\frac{x^2}{4}\right]_0^2=1')+para(bold('2. Accumulate area to a cutoff:'))+eq(r'F(x)=\begin{cases}0&x<0\\x^2/4&0\le x\le2\\1&x>2\end{cases}')+para(bold('3. Use the CDF for a left or right event:'))+eq(r'P(X<1)=\frac{1^2}{4}=.25')+eq(r'P(X>1.5)=1-\frac{1.5^2}{4}=.4375')+para(bold('4. Mean: value × density; integrate:'))+eq(r'E[X]=\int_0^2x\frac{x}{2}\,dx=\left[\frac{x^3}{6}\right]_0^2=\frac43')+para(bold('5. Mean square, then variance:'))+eq(r'E[X^2]=\int_0^2\frac{x^3}{2}\,dx=\left[\frac{x^4}{8}\right]_0^2=2')+eq(r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=2-\left(\frac43\right)^2=\frac29')+para(bold('6. Median: half the probability, not half the support:'))+eq(r'F(m)=\frac12\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{m^2}{4}=\frac12\ \Rightarrow\ m=\sqrt2')+para(r'Any exact point has zero probability. For intervals use \(F(b)-F(a)\). '+bold('Sketch:')+' rising straight-line density; CDF rises quadratically, then stays at one.'),'Session 4 pp. 20–21; review pp. 16–19')
block('endpoint','Unknown endpoint: normalize before using it',para(r'HW1: \(f(x)=0.1+x\) on \(0<x\le c\), zero elsewhere. '+bold('Find the supported area first:'))+eq(r'1=\int_0^c(.1+x)\,dx=.1c+\frac{c^2}{2}')+eq(r'c^2+.2c-2=0')+eq(r'c=\frac{-.2+\sqrt{.04+8}}2\approx1.317745')+para(r'The negative root does not fit positive support. Inside support:')+eq(r'F(x)=.1x+\frac{x^2}{2}')+para(r'Outside support use zero before the start and one after the end. '+bold('Power integration:'))+eq(r'\int x^k\,dx=\frac{x^{k+1}}{k+1}+C\quad(k\ne-1)')+para('Evaluate upper antiderivative minus lower antiderivative. A constant density is flat: probability is requested width ÷ full width.'),'HW1 Q3(a); Session 4 pp. 7, 19')
# Side 2: normal probability, repeated sampling, calculator and tables.
block('normal','Normal: shade → standardize → table',para(r'\(X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)\): mean \(\mu\), variance \(\sigma^2\), SD \(\sigma\).')+eq(r'z=\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma},\qquad\Phi(z)=P(Z\le z)')+eq(r'f(x)=\frac1{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\!\left[-\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\right]')+para(r'Use SD (not variance) in the denominator. The supplied z table lists positive \(z\) and left-tail areas.')+eq(r'\Phi(-z)=1-\Phi(z)')+eq(r'P(X>b)=1-\Phi(z_b)')+eq(r'P(a<X<b)=\Phi(z_b)-\Phi(z_a)')+para(r'\(z_a\) (standardized lower cutoff); \(z_b\) (standardized upper cutoff). Same Normal curve: mean = median; about 68%, 95%, 99.7% lies within one, two, three SDs. Density is bell-shaped; CDF rises smoothly from zero to one.'),'Review pp. 5, 20–22; Session 5')
block('normalwork','Glucose probabilities: all steps',para(r'HW1: \(\mu=95\), \(\sigma=8.7\) mg/dL. Find probabilities at cutoffs 85 and 115.')+eq(r'z_{85}=\frac{85-95}{8.7}=-1.149425\approx-1.15')+eq(r'z_{115}=\frac{115-95}{8.7}=2.298851\approx2.30')+para(bold('Read the supplied table (rounded z):'))+eq(r'\Phi(1.15)=.8749,\quad\Phi(2.30)=.9893')+eq(r'P(X<85)=1-.8749=.1251')+eq(r'P(85\le X<115)=.9893-.1251=.8642')+eq(r'P(X\ge115)=1-.9893=.0107')+eq(r'.1251+.8642+.0107=1')+para(r'Full-precision calculator checks: \(.125190,.864053,.0107567\). Table rounding explains the small differences. \(P(X=95)=0\) because this is continuous. The population variance is \(8.7^2=75.69\).'),'HW1 Q3(b); supplied z table')
block('inverse','Percentile: area → z → original units',para(r'Find the 97.5th percentile of the same glucose distribution. '+bold('1. Cumulative area; 2. z; 3. reverse standardization:'))+eq(r'p=.975,\quad z_p=1.96')+eq(r'x_p=\mu+z_p\sigma=95+1.96(8.7)')+eq(r'x_{.975}\approx112.052\ \text{mg/dL}')+para(r'For quartiles, \(z_{.25}\approx-.67449\), \(z_{.75}\approx.67449\): cutoffs \(89.1319\) and \(100.8681\) mg/dL. '+bold('Direction:')+' cutoff given → area; probability given → inverse cutoff. Above-median percentiles must exceed the mean.'),'HW1 Q3(b); Session 5 p. 9')
block('sampling','Average of measurements: use SEM',para(r'\(X\) is one measurement; \(\bar X\) is the mean of an entire sample. Repeated samples give different means. Their center stays at \(\mu\), but their spread shrinks:')+eq(r'E[\bar X]=\mu,\quad\operatorname{Var}(\bar X)=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}')+eq(r'\operatorname{SD}(\bar X)=\frac\sigma{\sqrt n}')+para(r'If individuals are Normal, means are Normal for any \(n\). Otherwise the course’s CLT guideline uses sufficiently large independent samples (\(n\ge30\)). '+bold('Lecture impurity example:')+r' \(\mu=4\), \(\sigma=1.5\) grams, \(n=50\); find \(P(3.5<\bar X<3.8)\).')+eq(r'\mathrm{SEM}=\frac{1.5}{\sqrt{50}}=.212132')+eq(r'z_L=\frac{3.5-4}{.212132}=-2.35702')+eq(r'z_U=\frac{3.8-4}{.212132}=-.942809')+eq(r'P=\Phi(-.942809)-\Phi(-2.35702)')+eq(r'\approx.172889-.00921106=.163678')+para('With z-table rounding: use −2.36 and −0.94; approximate result .1645. '+bold('Mean in the question → SEM in the denominator.')),'Session 5 p. 14; review pp. 23–31')
block('linear','What changes the center or uncertainty?',para(r'Adding a constant shifts the center but leaves spread unchanged. Multiplying stretches SD by the coefficient magnitude and variance by its square.')+eq(r'E[aX+bY+c]=aE[X]+bE[Y]+c')+eq(r'\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY+c)=a^2\sigma_X^2+b^2\sigma_Y^2')+para(r'The variance sum requires independent \(X,Y\). Subtracting an independent measurement still adds uncertainty; do not subtract its variance. '+bold('Sample-size shortcut:')+r' multiply \(n\) by four to halve SEM. '+bold('Variance-estimate uncertainty (Normal setting):'))+eq(r'\widehat{\operatorname{SE}}(s^2)=s^2\sqrt{\frac2{n-1}}')+para(r'\(s\) (individual SD); \(s/\sqrt n\) (SE of the mean); the expression above is SE of the variance. Unbiased means correct over repeated samples, not exact in one sample.'),'Session 8 pp. 6–8; review pp. 27, 30–31')
block('tables','Read THESE supplied statistical tables',para(bold('z table:')+r' left-tail area for positive \(z\). Locate the whole decimal z value in the repeated column pairs; use symmetry for negative z. Example \(z=.05\) gives \(.5199\). '+bold('t table:')+r' row \(\nu\) (df), column total two-tail \(\alpha\). Do not halve alpha again for this table. At \(\nu=4\), column \(.05\) gives \(2.776\). For two-sided z, the infinity-df row gives \(1.960\). '+bold('F table:')+r' numerator df across the top, denominator df down the side. Lightface is \(.05\), boldface \(.01\); select the matching line. '+bold('χ² table:')+r' row df, upper-tail column alpha. At \(.05\), df 1 gives \(3.841\), df 2 gives \(5.991\). '+bold('Missing df:')+' bracket using the neighboring available rows; a result beyond both bounds has an unambiguous decision. Use the provided conventions for any tighter case. '+bold('Pack separately:')+' original unannotated z, t, F, χ² tables; table pages do not count against the four notes sides.'),'Uploaded z, t, F, chi-square tables; review transcript')
block('ti','TI-84: only the useful non-basic entries',para(bold('Raw summaries:')+r' STAT → EDIT → enter observations into L1. STAT → CALC → 1-Var Stats, List=L1, FreqList blank (or 1), Calculate. Read \(\bar x\), '+bold('Sx')+r' (sample SD), and \(n\). The displayed '+bold('σx')+' instead divides by the list size; it is not the sample SD. '+bold('Normal area:')+' 2nd → VARS (DISTR) → normalcdf; lower=85, upper=115, μ=95, σ=8.7 → Paste/ENTER. For the sample-mean impurity example enter lower=3.5, upper=3.8, μ=4, σ=1.5/√50. '+bold('Normal percentile:')+' DISTR → invNorm; Area=.975, μ=95, σ=8.7, LEFT tail if offered. '+bold('One-sample t check:')+' STAT → TESTS → T-Test → Stats: μ0=72, x̄=76.8, Sx=4.15, n=5, ≠μ0 → Calculate. '+bold('Two-sample t check:')+' 2-SampTTest → Stats, enter the two means/SDs/sizes, ≠, '+bold('Pooled: Yes')+'. '+bold('Use parentheses around the whole denominator.')+' Keep hand setup/df/conclusion visible. '+bold('Do not replace the course’s corrected 2×2 work with an uncorrected calculator test.')),'TI-84 Plus CE Reference Guide; lecture inputs')
# Side 3: mean tests and follow-ups.
block('decision','Write an exam answer like this',para(bold('1. Test + H₀. 2. Labeled inputs. 3. Substitute and calculate. 4. df + table cutoff. 5. Decision in context.')+r' Two-sided t uses \(|t|\); F and χ² use their upper tail. Reject when the appropriate statistic exceeds its critical value, or when \(p<\alpha\). “Fail to reject” means a difference was not established, not that equality was proved. '+bold('Assumptions for course mean tests:')+' interval outcome, independent random samples, Normal populations; pooled t/ANOVA additionally assume equal population variances.'),'Review transcript; review pp. 33–45')
block('chooser','Which method fits the question?',para(bold('One numerical sample + fixed reference:')+' one-sample t. '+bold('Two independent numerical groups:')+' pooled Student t (sizes can differ). '+bold('Three or more balanced numerical groups:')+' ANOVA. '+bold('After significant ANOVA:')+' requested pairs using ANOVA within variance and denominator df. '+bold('Two event proportions:')+' Yates-corrected z or 2×2 χ². '+bold('Larger group × outcome table:')+' uncorrected contingency χ². '+bold('One count list + target proportions:')+' goodness-of-fit χ². '+bold('Magnitude of association:')+' prospective event risks → RR; case-control odds → OR.'),'Review decision chart and transcript')
block('anova','Balanced ANOVA: supplied means and SDs',para(r'\(m\) (groups), \(n\) (subjects per group). '+bold('H₀:')+r' population means equal. '+bold('HW1:')+r' \(m=3,n=12\), means \(1188,1412,1363\), SDs \(189,217,191\). '+bold('1. Square SDs; average variances:'))+eq(r's_{\mathrm{wit}}^2=\frac{189^2+217^2+191^2}{3}')+eq(r'=\frac{35721+47089+36481}{3}=39763.6667')+para(bold('2. Grand mean of the group means:'))+eq(r'\bar{\bar x}=\frac{1188+1412+1363}{3}=1321')+para(bold('3. Subtract grand mean; square and add:'))+eq(r'(1188-1321)^2+(1412-1321)^2+(1363-1321)^2')+eq(r'=(-133)^2+91^2+42^2=27734')+para(bold('4. Variance of means; multiply by group size:'))+eq(r's_{\bar x}^2=\frac{27734}{3-1}=13867')+eq(r's_{\mathrm{bet}}^2=n s_{\bar x}^2=12(13867)=166404')+para(bold('5. Divide; 6. df; 7. table; 8. conclude:'))+eq(r'F=\frac{166404}{39763.6667}=4.18483')+eq(r'\nu_1=m-1=2,\quad\nu_2=m(n-1)=33')+para(r'The supplied F table brackets the .05 critical value between \(3.23\) (df 40) and \(3.32\) (df 30). The statistic exceeds both: reject equal population means. Exact check: cutoff \(3.28492\), \(p=.024001\). '+bold('Short route:')+' average variances; variance of means × n; divide. ANOVA alone does not identify the pair.'),'HW1 Q5(a); review pp. 33–36')
block('pooled','Pooled t: two independent means',para(r'\(s_p^2\) (common estimated variance); \(\operatorname{SE}_{\Delta}\) (uncertainty of the mean difference).')+eq(r's_p^2=\frac{(n_1-1)s_1^2+(n_2-1)s_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}_{\Delta}=\sqrt{s_p^2(1/n_1+1/n_2)}')+eq(r't=\frac{\bar x_1-\bar x_2}{\operatorname{SE}_{\Delta}},\quad\nu=n_1+n_2-2')+para(bold('Lecture glucose:')+r' group 1 \((\bar x_1,s_1,n_1)=(82.2,2.49,25)\); group 2 \((86.1,2.09,25)\). '+bold('H₀:')+r' \(\mu_1=\mu_2\).')+eq(r's_1^2=6.2001,\quad s_2^2=4.3681')+eq(r's_p^2=\frac{24(6.2001)+24(4.3681)}{48}=5.2841')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}_{\Delta}=\sqrt{5.2841(1/25+1/25)}=.650175')+eq(r't=\frac{82.2-86.1}{.650175}=-5.99838')+eq(r'\nu=48,\quad |t|\approx6.00>2.011')+para('Reject equal population means at .05. Control mean is lower. The sign describes direction; magnitude makes the two-sided decision. '+bold('Equal-size shortcut:')+' pooled variance is the average of the two variances.'),'Session 8 pp. 15–16; review pp. 38–40')
block('unequal','Unequal sizes: keep the df weights',para(r'Lecture cardiac index: group 1 \((2.08,1.05,9)\), group 2 \((1.75,.88,16)\) in mean, SD, n order.')+eq(r'\nu=9+16-2=23')+eq(r's_p^2=\frac{8(1.05^2)+15(.88^2)}{23}')+eq(r'=\frac{8.82+11.616}{23}=.888522')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}_{\Delta}=\sqrt{.888522(1/9+1/16)}=.392756')+eq(r't=\frac{2.08-1.75}{.392756}=.840216')+para(r'At df 23, two-tail .05 cutoff is \(2.069\); fail to reject. '+bold('Shortcut warning:')+' unequal sizes cannot use a simple unweighted average of variances.'),'Session 8 p. 18')
block('onet','One-sample t: fixed reference, not group 2',para(r'Lecture HR: \(\bar x=76.8\), \(s=4.15\), \(n=5\); reference \(\mu_0=72\) BPM. '+bold('H₀:')+r' \(\mu_{\mathrm{HR}}=72\).')+eq(r'\mathrm{SEM}=\frac{s}{\sqrt n}=\frac{4.15}{\sqrt5}=1.855936')+eq(r'\bar x-\mu_0=76.8-72=4.8')+eq(r't=\frac{4.8}{1.855936}=2.58630')+eq(r'\nu=n-1=4,\quad t_{\mathrm{crit}}=2.776')+para(r'\(2.58630<2.776\): fail to reject at .05; this small sample does not establish a population mean different from 72 BPM. '+bold('Short route:')+' difference from reference ÷ SEM. The fixed reference has no second sample variance.'),'Session 8 pp. 22–23; review pp. 41–42')
block('pairs','After ANOVA: retain its shared noise and df',para(r'For requested groups \(i,j\), use the ANOVA within variance:')+eq(r'|t_{ij}|=\frac{|\bar x_i-\bar x_j|}{\sqrt{s_{\mathrm{wit}}^2(1/n_i+1/n_j)}}')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}_{ij}=\sqrt{2s_{\mathrm{wit}}^2/n}\quad(\text{equal }n)')+eq(r'\nu=N-m=m(n-1)')+para(r'HDL lecture: \(n=70\), \(s_{\mathrm{wit}}^2=239.9\), \(\nu=207\); means \(64.8,58.0,43.3\).')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}_{ij}=\sqrt{2(239.9)/70}=2.61807')+eq(r'|t_{13}|=\frac{64.8-43.3}{2.61807}=8.21216')+eq(r'|t_{23}|=\frac{58.0-43.3}{2.61807}=5.61483')+eq(r'|t_{12}|=\frac{64.8-58.0}{2.61807}=2.59733')+para(r'All pairs: \(k=m(m-1)/2=3\). Against inactive control only: \(k=m-1=2\). Use the requested family, not automatically all pairs.'),'Session 9 pp. 10–14; review p. 43')
block('multiplicity','Bonferroni versus Holm: the decision sequence',para(bold('Bonferroni:')+' same strict level for every requested pair.')+eq(r'\alpha_{\mathrm{pair}}=\alpha_T/k')+para(r'For three pairs and overall .05: \(\alpha_{\mathrm{pair}}=.016667\). In the supplied '+bold('two-tail t table')+', use that total two-tail level; an inverse-t command instead uses left area:')+eq(r'1-\frac{.05}{2(3)}=.9916667')+para(bold('Holm:')+' sort smallest p first (or largest statistic magnitude with the same df), keep labels, then:')+eq(r'\alpha_j=\frac{\alpha_T}{k-j+1}')+eq(r'k=3:\quad .05/3,\ .05/2,\ .05')+para(bold('Reject → continue; first failure → stop all remaining.')+' The HDL lecture’s all-pair magnitudes 8.2, 5.6, 2.6 pass the successive cutoffs and identify all three mean pairs. Against-control only uses two comparisons and a different family. '+bold('Do not use new two-group df for post-ANOVA pairs.')),'Session 9; review pp. 44–45')
# Side 4: nominal tests, effects and supplied-output use.
block('binary','Event proportions: label the numerator',para(r'\(m\) (events), \(n\) (total); code event=1, non-event=0.')+eq(r'\hat p=\frac mn,\quad\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(X)=\hat p(1-\hat p)')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}(\hat p)=\sqrt{\frac{\hat p(1-\hat p)}n}')+para(r'Use the same event in both groups. For death, count deaths; for thrombus, count thrombi. Binary variance and proportion SE are different: divide the former by n and square-root to obtain the latter.'),'Review p. 47; Session 11')
block('z','Aspirin/placebo: corrected z, every step',para(r'Placebo: 18 thrombi/25; aspirin: 6/19. '+bold('H₀:')+r' equal thrombus proportions. '+bold('1. Fractions; 2. pool actual counts:'))+eq(r'\hat p_1=18/25=.72,\quad\hat p_2=6/19=.315789')+eq(r'\hat p=\frac{18+6}{25+19}=\frac{24}{44}=.545455')+para(bold('3. Build the null SE:'))+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}_0=\sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)(1/n_1+1/n_2)}')+eq(r'=\sqrt{.545455(.454545)(1/25+1/19)}')+eq(r'=\sqrt{.0229665}=.151547')+para(bold('4. Shrink the absolute gap by Yates:'))+eq(r'c_Y=\tfrac12(1/25+1/19)=.0463158')+eq(r'D_Y=\max(0,|.72-.315789|-.0463158)')+eq(r'=.357895')+para(bold('5. Divide; 6. table and conclusion:'))+eq(r'|z_Y|=.357895/.151547=2.36161')+para(r'Infinity-df row of two-tail t table, alpha .05: \(1.960\). Reject equal event proportions: aspirin group has fewer thrombi. '+bold('Always use this correction for the course’s 2×2 tests.')),'Session 11 pp. 19–21; review pp. 49–50')
block('chi2','Same 2×2 table: expected cells and χ²',para(r'Rows placebo/aspirin; columns thrombus/no thrombus. Observed rows \((18,7)\), \((6,13)\); column totals \((24,20)\), row totals \((25,19)\), total \(44\).')+eq(r'E_{ij}=\frac{R_i C_j}{N}')+eq(r'\begin{array}{c|rr} &\text{thrombus}&\text{no thrombus}\\\text{placebo}&25(24)/44&25(20)/44\\\text{aspirin}&19(24)/44&19(20)/44\end{array}')+eq(r'E=\begin{pmatrix}13.63636&11.36364\\10.36364&8.63636\end{pmatrix}')+para('Each absolute gap is 4.363636; corrected gap is 3.863636. Square once, divide by each expected count:')+eq(r'\chi_Y^2=\sum\frac{\max(0,|O-E|-.5)^2}{E}')+eq(r'=1.094697+1.313636+1.440391+1.728469')+eq(r'=5.577193,\quad\nu=(2-1)(2-1)=1')+para(r'At alpha .05 cutoff \(3.841\): reject. Arithmetic check: \(2.36161^2\approx5.57719\). '+bold('Do not subtract .5 after squaring.')),'Session 12 p. 11; supplied chi-square table')
block('large','Larger contingency table: no Yates',para(r'Depression lecture observed rows: \((36,131)\), \((41,132)\), \((75,104)\); row totals \(167,173,179\), column totals \(152,367\), grand total \(519\). '+bold('H₀:')+' same outcome distribution across treatments.')+eq(r'E_{11}=167(152)/519=48.90944')+eq(r'E=\begin{pmatrix}48.90944&118.09056\\50.66667&122.33333\\52.42389&126.57611\end{pmatrix}')+eq(r'\chi^2=\sum_{i,j}\frac{(O_{ij}-E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}')+eq(r'=3.407393+1.411236+1.844298+.763851')+eq(r'\phantom{=}+9.722297+4.026673=21.175749')+eq(r'\nu=(3-1)(2-1)=2')+para(r'At .05, critical \(5.991\); reject the common-distribution null. Follow-ups use the requested 2×2 pairs, each with Yates, and a family correction. '+bold('Expected margins must match observed margins.')),'Session 12 pp. 14–15')
block('gof','Goodness of fit: one list vs target proportions',para(r'Exercise categories: observed \(255,125,90\), total \(N=470\); target probabilities \(.60,.25,.15\). '+bold('H₀:')+' category probabilities equal these targets.')+eq(r'E_i=Np_i\quad\Rightarrow\quad E=(282,117.5,70.5)')+eq(r'\chi^2=\frac{(255-282)^2}{282}+\frac{(125-117.5)^2}{117.5}')+eq(r'\phantom{\chi^2=}+\frac{(90-70.5)^2}{70.5}')+eq(r'=2.585106+.478723+5.393617=8.457447')+eq(r'\nu=k-1=2,\quad8.457447>5.991')+para('Reject the target distribution at .05. '+bold('Expected = total × specified probability')+'; do not calculate expected cells from new row/column margins for this one-list question.'),'Session 12 pp. 23–24; review pp. 54–55')
block('risk','Quantify size: RR, ARR, NNT, OR',para(r'For aspirin/placebo thrombi, treatment risk \(r_T=6/19=.315789\), control risk \(r_C=18/25=.72\).')+eq(r'\mathrm{RR}=\frac{r_T}{r_C}=\frac{6/19}{18/25}=.438596')+para('Aspirin’s thrombus risk is about .44 times placebo risk; it is not a 44-percentage-point reduction.')+eq(r'\mathrm{ARR}=r_C-r_T=.72-.315789=.404211')+eq(r'\mathrm{NNT}=1/\mathrm{ARR}=2.47396')+para('About 40.42 percentage points lower absolute risk; whole-person NNT rounds up to 3. For adverse-event increase, NNH is one divided by that increase. '+bold('Prospective trial/cohort:')+' group risks are estimable. '+bold('Case-control:')+' cases selected by outcome; use odds, not sample disease risk. Smoke lecture: cases exposed/unexposed 50/14; controls 43/35.')+eq(r'\mathrm{OR}=\frac{50/14}{43/35}=\frac{50(35)}{14(43)}=2.90698')+para('Exposure odds are about 2.91 times as high among cases as controls. Risk uses events/all; odds uses events/non-events.'),'Session 13 pp. 3–14; review pp. 56, 58–62')
block('output','Supplied output → continue the analysis',para(bold('No code writing on the exam.')+' Identify test, df, statistic and p. ANOVA output: within/residual mean square supplies the variance for mean follow-ups; use its residual df. t output: identify one sample vs pooled two samples, then compare p with alpha. χ² output: identify corrected 2×2 vs larger table, retain the pair labels. '+bold('HW2 output: control comparisons only')+r' have \(\chi^2=18.234,9.4944,4.6895\), ordered strongest first (G3, G1, G2). Three comparisons, each df 1:')+eq(r'\alpha_j=.05/3,\ .05/2,\ .05')+para('Compare each against its upper-tail χ² cutoff (bracket the first with available table columns); all three pass. Do not use all six printed pairs when only three control comparisons are requested. '+bold('If a step fails, Holm stops.')),'Review transcript; HW2 Q3 output; review pp. 53, 63')
block('trial','Design and quick interpretation checks',para(bold('Randomized clinical trial:')+' assigns treatments, follows outcomes (prospective). '+bold('Prospective cohort:')+' no randomization; follow exposure groups. '+bold('Retrospective case-control:')+' select people with/without outcome, look back at exposure. Randomization concerns assignment; masking concerns who knows it. '+bold('Association is not automatically causation.')+' Ask separately: is a difference established (test), and how large is it (RR/OR/absolute change)? '+bold('Bring:')+' calculator, four printed notes sides, and separate unannotated z/t/F/χ² tables. Session 14 and manual sum-of-squares ANOVA are excluded.'),'Sessions 10, 13; review transcript')

block('uniform','Uniform: reuse width, area and moments',para(r'\(a\) (lower endpoint), \(b\) (upper endpoint). A flat density must cover total area one:')+eq(r'f(x)=\frac1{b-a}\quad(a<x\le b)')+eq(r'F(x)=\frac{x-a}{b-a}\quad(a<x\le b)')+eq(r'E[X]=\frac{a+b}{2},\quad\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}')+para(r'Lecture standard Uniform support: \(0<x\le1\). Then \(f(x)=1\) and inside the support:')+eq(r'F(x)=\int_0^x1\,du=x')+eq(r'E[X]=\int_0^1x\,dx=\left[x^2/2\right]_0^1=1/2')+eq(r'E[X^2]=\int_0^1x^2\,dx=\left[x^3/3\right]_0^1=1/3')+eq(r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac13-\left(\frac12\right)^2=\frac1{12}')+para('Short route for any interval inside support: requested width divided by full width. CDF is zero before support and one after it.'),'Session 4 pp. 7, 19')
block('cltwhy','Why the mean’s denominator changes',para(r'For \(n\) independent measurements, each with variance \(\sigma^2\), add their uncertainty contributions first. Averaging then divides the sum by \(n\), and variance scales by the square of that factor:')+eq(r'\operatorname{Var}\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i\right)=n\sigma^2')+eq(r'\operatorname{Var}(\bar X)=\left(\frac1n\right)^2n\sigma^2=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}')+eq(r'\operatorname{SD}(\bar X)=\sqrt{\sigma^2/n}=\sigma/\sqrt n')+para(r'If only the sample is available, substitute \(s\) (sample SD) to estimate SEM. This does not turn individual spread into a smaller SD; it describes a different random quantity, the mean. '+bold('ANOVA connection:')+' variance among group means is on a mean-error scale. Multiplying it by common group size brings it back to the individual-variance scale before comparing with within variance.'),'Sessions 8, 10; review transcript; review p. 27')
block('anovaoutput','Read real ANOVA output: nitric oxide',eq(r'\begin{array}{l|rrr}\text{row}&\text{df}&\text{Mean Sq}&F\\\text{Group}&2&389.7&5.832\\\text{Residuals}&19&66.8&\end{array}')+para(r'The printed Group-row p value is \(.0106\). '+bold('1. Test:')+' overall population means. '+bold('2. Decision:')+r' \(.0106<.05\), so reject equal means. '+bold('3. If asked to continue:')+r' Residual Mean Sq supplies within variance \(66.8\); residual df is \(19\). '+bold('4. Pairwise family:')+' use requested groups and the stated correction; do not replace residual df with a new two-group df. '+bold('No manual sum-of-squares calculation is needed to read this table.')+' The two mean-square entries are printed rounded, so their ratio need only agree approximately with the reported F.'),'Session 7 p. 39, actual R output')
block('proportionwork','Binary SE: keep the two spreads separate',para(r'Halothane example: 8 deaths among 61 patients. The event is death. '+bold('1. Estimate proportion; 2. binary variance:'))+eq(r'\hat p=\frac8{61}=.131148')+eq(r'\hat p(1-\hat p)=\frac8{61}\frac{53}{61}=.113948')+para(bold('3. Individual binary SD; 4. SE of the proportion:'))+eq(r'\widehat{\operatorname{SD}}(X)=\sqrt{.113948}=.337562')+eq(r'\operatorname{SE}(\hat p)=\sqrt{.113948/61}=.0432203')+para('A larger n reduces uncertainty in the proportion. If everyone has the event or nobody does, the binary observations have no spread. The displayed calculation is recomputed from counts, rather than a conflicting printed intermediate value.'),'Session 11 p. 12; review p. 47')
# Make calculator instructions short, sequential entries rather than one paragraph.
BLOCKS['ti']['html']=para(bold('Raw summaries:')+' STAT → EDIT → enter data in L1. To clear old entries, highlight the L1 name, CLEAR, ENTER. STAT → CALC → 1-Var Stats; List=L1; frequency blank/1; Calculate. Read x̄, '+bold('Sx (sample SD)')+' and n. σx uses the list-size denominator; do not substitute it for sample SD.')+para(bold('Normal area:')+' 2nd → VARS (DISTR) → normalcdf. For glucose: lower 85; upper 115; μ 95; σ 8.7; Paste/ENTER. For the mean impurity: lower 3.5; upper 3.8; μ 4; σ 1.5/√50. That final input is SEM.')+para(bold('Percentile:')+' DISTR → invNorm: area .975; μ 95; σ 8.7; LEFT tail if offered. For a tail extending to infinity, use the calculator’s practical bound ±1E99, with E entered using 2nd → comma (EE).')+para(bold('One-sample t check:')+' STAT → TESTS → T-Test → Stats: μ0 72; x̄ 76.8; Sx 4.15; n 5; choose ≠; Calculate.')+para(bold('Pooled two-sample check:')+' 2-SampTTest → Stats: x̄1 82.2, Sx1 2.49, n1 25; x̄2 86.1, Sx2 2.09, n2 25; ≠; '+bold('Pooled: Yes')+'; Calculate.')+para(bold('Adjusted positive t cutoff:')+' DISTR → invT, left area 1−.05/(2k), df from the method. If invT is absent on that OS, use the t table. '+bold('Two-tail p after hand t:')+' DISTR → tcdf, lower=|t|, upper=1E99, df=method’s df; multiply the resulting tail area by two.')+para(bold('Retain intermediate values:')+' after pooled variance, STO→ A (A holds pooled variance). Build the SE using A; STO→ B (B holds SE). Then enter the mean difference divided by B. Keep A/B’s meanings written beside the work.')+eq(r'A=5.2841,\quad B=\sqrt{A(1/25+1/25)}')+eq(r't=(82.2-86.1)/B')+para(bold('Use the entire denominator in parentheses.')+' A calculator result does not replace the written null, setup, df, and conclusion. Standard two-proportion/χ² calculator routines may omit the required Yates correction; use the hand corrected recipe for those.')
